<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viroconium Cornoviorum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Viroconium, Uriconium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Viroconium Cornoviorum, at Wroxeter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, was the capital of the Cornovii in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and one of the largest Roman towns in the province. It grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of the mid-1st century CE into a civitas capital with a forum and great public baths. Unlike most Roman towns, it was rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and lived on into the 5th and 6th centuries, possibly as a seat of post-Roman rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viroconium-cornoviorum.jpg" alt="Viroconium Cornoviorum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alastair Rae from London, United Kingdom · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viroconium_Cornoviorum_08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies about 8 km east-south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, near the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a>, the main Roman road across Britannia from Dubris (Dover). It guarded the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> where it comes out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and the route south to the Wye valley. Its name is a Latinised form of a Common Brittonic place name reconstructed as *Uiroconion, the city of *Uirokū, a man's name meaning man-wolf; the Cornovii's original capital of that name was the hillfort on the Wrekin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first post held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohort</a> of Thracian auxiliaries during the campaigns of the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula">Publius Ostorius Scapula</a>. Legio XIV Gemina replaced it with a larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress ahead of the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a> took that legion north in 80, Legio XX Valeria Victrix held the fortress until it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Deva Victrix</a> (Chester) in the late 80s. The civilian settlement around the fort then grew into a town, and an unfinished legionary bath house became its forum.</p><p>The colonnaded forum, begun in the 120s, carried a dedicatory inscription to Emperor Hadrian dated to 130. The town came to cover more than 70 ha and is estimated to have had over 15,000 people, which made it the fourth-largest Roman settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Between 165 and 185 the forum burned down with neighbouring shops and houses and was rebuilt.</p><p>Urban life went on into the 5th century; the Cunorix Stone, a gravestone in partly Latinised Primitive Irish probably of 460-475, dates from then. Between 530 and 570 the old basilica was demolished and replaced by 33 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed buildings on rubble platforms, built to Roman measurements, perhaps on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s initiative. The site was probably abandoned peacefully in the later 7th or early 8th century, and in 656 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a>'s conquest made the area part of the Wreocensæte, a sub-kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A corner of the city is now the village of Wroxeter. English Heritage cares for the remains as Wroxeter Roman City, with a small museum; they include the Old Work, part of the baths' frigidarium and the largest free-standing Roman ruin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Most of the town is still buried but has been mapped by geophysical survey and aerial archaeology, and a reconstructed Roman villa opened in February 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viroconium Cornoviorum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Powys emerged in the fifth century in the lands of the Cornovii, a British people whose Roman civitas capital was <b>Viroconium</b>, the town now called Wroxeter near Shrewsbury.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most people lived in farmsteads and villages much like those of the Iron Age, but a network of towns grew up: coloniae of veterans at Colchester, Lincoln and Gloucester, later York, and civitas capitals for the native peoples, such as Verulamium (St Albans), Corinium (Cirencester) and <b>Viroconium</b> (Wroxeter).</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman army advanced into the region in the 50s, and around 55 to 60 a legionary fortress was built at <b>Wroxeter</b> below the Wrekin, which seems to have been abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornovii">Cornovii</a></li><li><blockquote>Its centre is uncertain; later tradition placed it at Shrewsbury, while scholars have suggested <b>Wroxeter</b>, Baschurch or the Wrekin.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-pengwern">Kingdom of Pengwern</a></li></ul></section>
